    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d386">

                <pb facs="0529" n="415" xml:id="pg415"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/4710: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Whitehouse</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-10-26T11:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 26,
                                1924—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 26—11:05
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>463. My 458, October 23, 7 p.m.<anchor n="36" xml:id="d386-f1"/> Léger of the
                    Asiatic Division informed me last evening by order of Laroche<anchor n="37"
                        xml:id="d386-f2"/> that the French attitude regarding the maintenance of the
                    integrity of the Canton customs had in no way been modified. French Government
                    was quite prepared to oppose seizure and considered that the instructions given
                    the French admiral for last December were continuing and would apply
                    automatically at any recurrence of the same situation. Léger was very definite
                    on this question for naval instructions although I had not mentioned either
                    warships or instructions in my conversation with Laroche.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Whitehouse</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
